WEBVTT

1
00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.910
"Cigan" and "Rom" in principle mean the same thing.

2
00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:16.830
And "rom" in Romani means "human being."

3
00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:21.770
So we, the Roma, are human beings too.

4
00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:28.690
Etymologically the word "Gypsy" comes from an ancient Greek word

5
00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.350
meaning "dirty," "leprous" or "untouchable."

6
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:39.850
It's believed that in fact 80 percent of the world's population uses this word in a derogatory, that is, offensive context,

7
00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:52.430
while 20% of the population, to which you who are here with me belong, most likely use it because they simply see no difference.

8
00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:55.390
It all depends on how each person perceives it.

9
00:02:56.770 --> 00:03:01.910
Whether with good intent or bad.

10
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:11.060
That is, if someone says "Gypsies," it can be mocking, and it can be kind.

11
00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:18.160
And Romani poetry has always existed and always will.

12
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:26.540
Most authors who used the word "Gypsy" or the adjective "Gypsy" did so with good intentions.

13
00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:34.880
So in this sense I really see no problem in using it in an artistic sense, context.

14
00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.060
I see no problem at all.

15
00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:45.560
But in official language the word that belongs to us and that we ourselves use should be used — "Rom."

16
00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.850
We remain ourselves because others don't want us.

17
00:04:17.710 --> 00:04:19.550
And because we are constantly rejected.

18
00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:21.250
That is why we remain ourselves.

19
00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:26.850
Through our culture, through our song, through our dance.

20
00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:33.850
By our way of life and by tradition.

21
00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:39.090
The majority of the population has formed an idea of some idyllic world.

22
00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:41.070
In this culture everything is possible.

23
00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:42.990
Like in Márquez's novels.

24
00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:44.930
Like in Latin American literature.

25
00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:53.330
That, in fact, is the essence of the whole story about the beauty of Roma existence.

26
00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.190
Remaining yourself is possible, feasible.

27
00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:03.830
But how much you pay for it — history shows best.

28
00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:17.730
And threats are the least of it.

29
00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:25.730
Not to mention genocides, persecutions, abuse, oppression, racism and everything else.

30
00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:56.060
Everyone who wants to be an author must find their own style.

31
00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.000
I was in this puzzle for several years.

32
00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:03.340
I had already decided to give up, to stop pursuing this idea,

33
00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.440
to go back to the faculty and finish it,

34
00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:11.340
to latch onto a normal, ordinary identity as soon as possible,

35
00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:13.800
rather than this desire to become a creator.

36
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:20.260
However, at the last moment, just when all the trains had left,

37
00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:25.940
a collection of poems, "Garavi sokak" by Mika Antić, came into my hands by chance,

38
00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:31.760
and after five poems I said: that's it.

39
00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:38.100
I met Mika Antić in '73,

40
00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:45.000
and my father Tugomir was friends with him, they had known each other for many years.

41
00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:50.340
And at that time, in '73, we were working in Čortanovci,

42
00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:58.740
and he had been given a bungalow there and was writing the book "Garavi sokak."

43
00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:08.880
In these supposedly children's poems

44
00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:11.380
there is an angle

45
00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:17.120
from which you can discover a completely new world,

46
00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:21.600
one you can't find elsewhere,

47
00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:24.100
a world that is very close to me,

48
00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:27.580
a world in which I feel at home,

49
00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:31.820
and I realized that this could become the basis of an aesthetic

50
00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:34.380
on which I could build a style

51
00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:40.560
that I lacked, as a link in the chain of the "Garavi sokak" structure.

52
00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:42.340
We talked a great deal,

53
00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:45.120
because every evening, as soon as evening came,

54
00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.380
he'd come to the kafana and sit with us, we'd drink there,

55
00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:53.200
and we played, he was extraordinarily fond of Roma music.

56
00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.860
And especially he loved my father — how he plays and how he sings.

57
00:08:56.860 --> 00:09:01.500
I was very young, I was 18 when I met Mika.

58
00:11:55.990 --> 00:12:02.470
Where does this love and friendship with the Roma, with the Gypsies come from?

59
00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:06.070
Simply street to street, they lived nearby,

60
00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:07.350
went to school together,

61
00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:10.630
were friends both in Mokrin and in Pančevo,

62
00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:15.930
and it was... they were simply born with this.

63
00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:19.410
I had extraordinarily good Roma friends,

64
00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:23.690
especially in childhood and youth.

65
00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:26.630
My experience is positive.

66
00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:33.500
I can't draw a distinction compared with the experience of dealing

67
00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:38.400
with members of other nations, faiths, skin colors and so on.

68
00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:42.860
And these friendships are truly wonderful.

69
00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:04.240
Because they knew how to convey to us

70
00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:08.600
this precious philosophy: you must live in the present moment.

71
00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:11.800
There were, there were, thank God.

72
00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:15.740
Many — both acquaintances and strangers.

73
00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:21.600
There, recently he came to see me, was a guest —

74
00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:26.140
unfortunately, he passed away some time ago —

75
00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:32.960
the last tamburitza player of the "Eight Tamburitza Players,"

76
00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:35.960
Moma Nikolić from Canada.

77
00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:44.760
He wanted to see me and visited here, in this studio.

78
00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:55.910
My grandmother Mela, Mika's mother, often

79
00:13:55.910 --> 00:13:57.890
said: Mika, you're such an inventor.

80
00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:03.590
But for an artist it's normal to invent, right?

81
00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:06.830
That's his job, but Mile Dileja really existed.

82
00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:08.190
He was Dad's school friend.

83
00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:11.690
Dad often defended him, because he was small.

84
00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:17.390
And, of course, spent time with him,

85
00:14:17.530 --> 00:14:19.490
a great deal of time, and actually admired him,

86
00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:21.070
because he was full of imagination.

87
00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:24.250
He could sing too, he was musical,

88
00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:28.490
and also made up some words.

89
00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:32.830
And that, I'd say, inspired Mika.

90
00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:36.070
And probably over the course of his life he often remembered him

91
00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:38.090
and tried in a way to be him.

92
00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:40.990
And at the same time we know that the Roma were

93
00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:43.910
among the greatest victims of World War II,

94
00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:47.070
and this unfortunate and talented boy was shot.

95
00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:50.550
And Mika definitely wanted one day

96
00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:54.670
to pay him a kind of tribute,

97
00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:58.470
to remind people how talented this people is.

98
00:14:59.110 --> 00:15:02.370
He mentioned Mile from time to time

99
00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:07.770
and often made very interesting jokes about it,

100
00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:11.150
but, I'd say, jealously guarded this story

101
00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:13.270
for precisely this book, "Garavi sokak."

102
00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:34.480
Just as Black people in America have their own style of music,

103
00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:39.820
so the Roma in Europe and here have a style of their own.

104
00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:44.720
The blues is nothing other than when a good person feels bad.

105
00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:48.440
It's the same with Roma music.

106
00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:49.540
Everything.

107
00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:52.200
Life, love, death.

108
00:16:52.820 --> 00:16:55.340
Where do these Gypsy tamburitza players come from?

109
00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:56.740
Where from?

110
00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:57.960
From an early age.

111
00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:02.060
Because it's inside him.

112
00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:07.420
He trembles when he plays.

113
00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:10.700
When we're happy, when we're sad,

114
00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:14.260
and when we love, and when we don't —

115
00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:16.740
the instruments are nearby.

116
00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:27.420
I can't sing it, of course.

117
00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:29.480
But I always request this song,

118
00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:30.580
and when they sing it,

119
00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:31.900
even though I don't understand

120
00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:33.960
what it's about,

121
00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:39.360
I feel what somehow touches them,

122
00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:40.760
and they pass it on to us.

123
00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:45.380
An incredible people, extraordinarily talented in music.

124
00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:48.640
Where this comes from and why — I can't explain.

125
00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:50.240
But that's how it is.

126
00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:52.120
These are some alchemical processes.

127
00:17:52.220 --> 00:17:53.940
Through this suffering of theirs

128
00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:57.600
they established music that is very sad,

129
00:17:58.020 --> 00:17:59.540
very sensual

130
00:17:59.540 --> 00:18:02.520
and very cheerful.

131
00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:12.760
And these are cheerful songs.

132
00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:14.380
These are songs that lift you up.

133
00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:30.140
They always ask: were there tamburitza players?

134
00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:30.840
There weren't?

135
00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:33.480
Then there was no celebration.

136
00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:35.260
My father always admired them,

137
00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:36.400
maybe I'd even say,

138
00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:38.180
maybe even envied them a little,

139
00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:43.020
the Roma knew how to

140
00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:44.720
turn a song into poetry

141
00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:45.400
and vice versa.

142
00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:46.500
They were full of imagination,

143
00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:48.460
they made things up, and that

144
00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:49.760
captivated him from an early age.

145
00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:54.200
Talent is talent.

146
00:18:58.040 --> 00:18:59.620
God endows some more,

147
00:18:59.620 --> 00:19:00.340
some less.

148
00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:04.200
A trade can be learned,

149
00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:06.860
but imagination can't.

150
00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:08.900
That can't be learned,

151
00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:10.800
you can't get a diploma for it.

152
00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:35.220
he had the chance to listen to the Roma Jovanovićs,

153
00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:36.520
but a

154
00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:40.500
actually Roma from Batajnica, the Ivanovićs,

155
00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:43.940
but "Ivanović" somehow sounded Russian.

156
00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:48.540
There was above all Žarko Jovanović,

157
00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:49.580
also known as Jagdino,

158
00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:52.400
who played the balalaika,

159
00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:53.160
he was a virtuoso,

160
00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:56.720
but there were also his sons,

161
00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:58.300
who played other instruments,

162
00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:00.660
and during the break they met my father

163
00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:02.420
and his wife

164
00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:06.900
and simply in that very second fell in love with each other.

165
00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:13.520
Mika had published his "Garavi sokak" a year earlier,

166
00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:17.500
and it had been heard of much farther, across the whole Roma community,

167
00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:20.720
and it was simply mutual adoration from the first second.

168
00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:22.400
Jagdino, by the way,

169
00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:23.460
was not only a virtuoso,

170
00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:26.680
he was also secretary

171
00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:30.960
of the World International Romani Federation

172
00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:35.620
and also designed their flag: blue sky,

173
00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:37.560
green grass and a wheel in the middle

174
00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:39.900
as a symbol of eternal movement.

175
00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:43.020
He also compiled a Romani dictionary,

176
00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:44.380
which he gave to my father,

177
00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:47.060
and he brought it to Novi Sad wanting to publish it,

178
00:20:47.060 --> 00:20:49.420
so that such a thing would appear for the first time.

179
00:20:50.180 --> 00:20:53.080
Besides, Žarko

180
00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:55.120
had various engagements,

181
00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:57.100
for example, he was greatly respected,

182
00:20:57.100 --> 00:21:00.240
and the city of Paris allowed him

183
00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:02.380
on the outskirts of the Bois de Boulogne

184
00:21:02.380 --> 00:21:04.480
to park his caravan in which he lived.

185
00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:06.360
When, say, in 1981

186
00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:08.240
my brother Jug was born,

187
00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:10.040
he came to Novi Sad,

188
00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:11.200
came to my father,

189
00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:12.460
presented him with an honorary

190
00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:16.360
membership card of that World Federation

191
00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:17.580
of Roma

192
00:21:17.820 --> 00:21:20.860
and, among other things, gave him a cassette

193
00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:24.160
of music of the Roma

194
00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:25.800
Ivanovićs,

195
00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:28.720
which my father took to RTS,

196
00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:31.460
and then this music was played in the New Year's program

197
00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:32.440
in prime time,

198
00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:33.520
and it became a real hit.

199
00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:36.260
Besides, my father advocated

200
00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:39.600
for creating, for the Roma,

201
00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:42.240
an association of composers,

202
00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:43.160
of musicians in general,

203
00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:44.640
here, in Novi Sad,

204
00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:45.920
and he succeeded,

205
00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:49.660
so that they could exercise their rights,

206
00:21:51.500 --> 00:21:53.120
their status and so on.

207
00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:54.860
That is, there was

208
00:21:54.860 --> 00:21:56.280
mutual respect,

209
00:21:56.340 --> 00:21:57.460
which lasted for years,

210
00:21:57.460 --> 00:22:00.440
and, of course, continued

211
00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:03.640
in listening to music,

212
00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:04.640
in carousing

213
00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:06.240
and in similar things.

214
00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:10.400
Extraordinarily beautiful music,

215
00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:12.080
films, works

216
00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:14.200
of classical literature —

217
00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.800
so many first-class artists

218
00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:19.380
were inspired by the Roma.

219
00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:23.660
In my life two Gypsies have amazed me.

220
00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:26.500
One from my own craft,

221
00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:29.700
and that's Pablo Picasso.

222
00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:34.910
The greatest painter of the 20th century.

223
00:22:35.290 --> 00:22:36.770
A fantastic draftsman.

224
00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:39.010
A painter — needless to say.

225
00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:40.870
A sculptor.

226
00:22:43.570 --> 00:22:45.950
He amazed the whole world.

227
00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:52.030
He is the history of 20th-century art.

228
00:22:56.870 --> 00:23:00.570
The second source of inspiration was Janika Balaž.

229
00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:06.170
When that song was being created,

230
00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:08.010
he told me,

231
00:23:08.110 --> 00:23:09.050
he says: Ratko,

232
00:23:10.630 --> 00:23:13.190
when we perform this,

233
00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:16.110
Petrovaradin will crack.

234
00:23:16.930 --> 00:23:19.350
I say this as an ordinary citizen,

235
00:23:19.350 --> 00:23:23.860
because I know what Mika Antić means.

236
00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:28.120
And a big monument should be put up to him somewhere in Novi Sad.

237
00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:31.600
If needed, we artists will help too,

238
00:23:31.780 --> 00:23:33.820
with paintings, to make something happen.

239
00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:05.580
I'm glad that this song

240
00:24:06.480 --> 00:24:08.460
went out among the people.

241
00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:14.240
Many think it's a folk song altogether.

242
00:24:14.720 --> 00:24:17.320
And that pleases me.

243
00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:15.200
Čohano means

244
00:26:15.200 --> 00:26:17.620
bogeyman, vampire.

245
00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:21.300
That is, when you want to scare children,

246
00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:24.060
you say

247
00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:26.400
that a vampire will come,

248
00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:28.500
that a witch will come and so on.

249
00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:30.920
Čohano is an unclean creature.

250
00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:32.700
The Roma generally

251
00:26:32.700 --> 00:26:34.980
have a sort of collective fears

252
00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:35.820
of various things.

253
00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:45.260
First of all they have, of course, like everyone else, a great fear of death.

254
00:26:45.640 --> 00:26:47.220
Then they have a collective fear

255
00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:49.000
of water.

256
00:26:49.880 --> 00:26:51.540
We are very weak swimmers.

257
00:26:52.060 --> 00:26:53.580
Also we have a collective fear

258
00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:55.500
of people in uniform.

259
00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:55.960
Why?

260
00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:57.200
Because we have always

261
00:26:57.200 --> 00:26:58.960
been guests.

262
00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:00.920
Everywhere we were guests.

263
00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:04.820
But the greatest fear —

264
00:27:04.820 --> 00:27:06.160
is the fear of

265
00:27:06.160 --> 00:27:07.280
čohano.